WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.620
I'm Kim Bhasin, luxury
reporter at Bloomberg,

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.520
here to invite you into
the most exclusive realms

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.713
of luxury retail.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.463
This is Invitation Only.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.890
Today, I'm in Chelsea,
the Manhattan neighborhood

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.490
with everything a fancy
person could ask for.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.840
Tons of super expensive art.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.510
A bustling, pricey market.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.860
And a formally posh hotel
turned historic landmark.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.380
Once home to notable fancy
guests like Lillie Langtry,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.703
Milos Forman, Jane
Fonda, and Bette Midler.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.050
So with its knack at preserving
old beautiful things,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.460
it shouldn't be a surprise
that Chelsea is also home

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.340
to the world's fanciest
used clothing shop,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:49.728
New York Vintage.

00:00:49.728 --> 00:00:52.500
(doorbell rings)

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.600
Hi.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.433
Hello.

00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:55.569
Hi, welcome.

00:00:55.569 --> 00:00:56.402
How are you?

00:00:56.402 --> 00:00:57.568
I'm good, how are you?

00:00:57.568 --> 00:00:58.401
Great!

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.420
So we're in our retail store
right now, the bottom floor,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.620
which is open to the public and
anybody can come in and shop

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:09.013
and purchase these wonderful,
rare pieces of vintage,

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.320
and then the second floor
is a private showroom

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.320
that houses over a 150
years of important fashion.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.540
Balenciaga, Halston,
Schiaparelli, Fortuny, Worth,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.800
really special, rare pieces
that are museum caliber,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.400
basically impossible to come by.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.310
Downstairs, all these pieces are for sale.

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:31.690
Upstairs they're for rent.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:32.690
Who shops up there?

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.640
Who's allowed up in the treasure trove?

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.780
We work with editors and
stylists for industry use,

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.900
but we have a lot of our
private clientele, celebrities

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.290
that will come up for fittings
for various events as well.

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.150
What kind of celebrities?

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.540
Um, every kind. (laughs)

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.670
The A-list kind.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.200
Do your dresses show up on the red carpet?

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.400
Oh all the time, absolutely, yeah.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.480
And to see the clothing kind
of take on a different life

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:03.483
is incredible.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:08.200
To get an appointment upstairs,
can anyone just walk in

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.780
or call you and get an
appointment to go check it out?

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.260
It's not that easy. (laughs)

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:13.890
You have to have credentials.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:14.723
Do you vet people?

00:02:14.723 --> 00:02:15.556
We vet people.

00:02:15.556 --> 00:02:17.020
Everyone is vetted before they have access

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.270
to the archive, absolutely.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.520
Am I allowed up there?

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.390
Um, I'll let you have a peek. (laughs)

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:23.068
I'll give you a little guided tour.

00:02:23.068 --> 00:02:26.485
(upbeat, rhythmic music)

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:30.650
Oh, look at this.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.090
Ready to be inspired?

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:33.923
Uh-huh.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.110
So over here we have an
Adrian, this is 1930s.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.180
He designed costumes for over 250 films

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.840
during Hollywood's Golden Era.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.060
Jean Harlow, Joan Crawford, and Garbo,

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.130
all of these ladies wore his gowns.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:52.600
He's known for this silhouette,
the bias 1930s dress.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.475
These are corsets from the 1880s.

00:02:54.475 --> 00:02:55.720
1880s?

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.370
Uh-huh, 1880s.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.910
We have some examples of
some McQueen headpieces.

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.323
Over here we have Gianni Versace.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.890
Back here we have a bra
that was worn by Lady Gaga.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:10.690
I believe this was just for an editorial.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.840
Just to wear, 'cause she's
Lady Gaga, of course.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.140
And by the way, this
belonged to Josephine Baker,

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.100
so this bra is from the 1920s,

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.170
a lot of famous breasts were... (laughs)

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.050
At this rack.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.700
Well at this rack (laughing)...

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.877
So this is the famous
Sarah Jessica Parker,

00:03:27.877 --> 00:03:30.150
"Sex and the City" bird.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.240
(dramatic orchestra music)

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.480
Has anyone tried to buy this off of you?

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.070
I would say the most
interesting solicitation was

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:42.490
from a Saudi princess who
was buying up everything

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.590
from "Sex and the City" the film,

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.730
and offered a very large sum of money.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.080
Of course we turned it down--

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.340
How large is large?

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.173
Six figures.

00:03:50.173 --> 00:03:51.697
Oh, for this bird.

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:53.047
Yes for this little bird.

00:03:55.393 --> 00:03:58.226
(slow, deep bass)

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.630
I've had some moments where
I've realized the impact

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:04.493
we've had on the fashion industry.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.660
I can share an experience
a few years ago where I was

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.260
up in my showroom just working away.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.110
I had my three children running around me

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.817
and I get a call on my cell
phone from the White House,

00:04:14.817 --> 00:04:17.637
"We'd like to put Michelle
Obama in vintage."

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.310
Just never knowing what or
who you're working with,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.950
knowing that somehow you
inspired someone to take an idea

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:27.943
and to run with it.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.030
So you're hearing all this talk

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:36.110
about the retail
apocalypse, retail is dying,

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.660
how is that affecting this business?

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.390
It's so scary, I've been
here for almost 20 years

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.550
and I don't think that anybody on my block

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.030
is really standing since we first opened,

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.100
and I think that the reason
why we haven't really

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.220
been impacted is because
it is an experience

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.700
when you come here.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.610
We've been asked by schools
to have private tours,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.020
we've opened our archive
to students for research,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.100
you just feel good coming in and you feel

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.564
like you've walked into a time
capsule of fashion history.

00:05:05.564 --> 00:05:07.453
(laughs) All in one place.

